{
  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "esther-uninvited",
  "text": "Walking into that throne room uninvited was a death sentence, and she put on the crown and did it anyway.\n\nOne law in Persia. Man or woman, you come to the king unsummoned, you die. Unless he holds out the golden sceptre.\n\nAnd Esther tells Mordecai the part that had to keep her up at night: \"I have not been called to come in unto the king these thirty days.\"\n\nThirty days. She's the queen. She has no idea where she stands.\n\nAnd Mordecai's reply is brutal and kind at the same time. \"Think not with thyself that thou shalt escape in the king's house, more than all the Jews.\"\n\nYou are not safe. The palace is not a shelter.\n\nThen: if you stay silent, deliverance will arise from another place, but you and your father's house will be destroyed.\n\nHear that theology. He never says God will fail. He says rescue is coming and you might miss it.\n\n\"And who knoweth whether thou art come to the kingdom for such a time as this?\"\n\nA question. Not a command. He leaves her free.\n\nSo she fasts three days and sends back, \"If I perish, I perish.\"\n\nThird day, royal apparel, inner court. And the king holds out the sceptre.\n\nShe lives. And then she throws the dinner party that flips an empire.",
